5. CREDIT-KIND SIGN OVERRIDE — PASS, and the tests genuinely discriminate
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Implementation, loader.go:491-514:

  forcedByKind := acct != nil && acct.Kind == models.AccountKindCredit
  if forcedByKind || usesCreditCardSignConvention(transactions) {
      ... for i := range transactions {
              transactions[i].Amount = -transactions[i].Amount
              transactions[i].Hash = transactions[i].ComputeHash()
          }
  }
  // Attribution: after parsing and the sign decision, before dedup.
  if acct != nil && acct.ID != "" { for i := range transactions {
      transactions[i].AccountID = acct.ID } }

`git diff -U0` on loader.go shows the heuristic itself is untouched: the only lines
mentioning usesCreditCardSignConvention / minSignConventionSample /
ccConventionPositiveThreshold in the whole diff are the single condition line
(`- if usesCreditCardSignConvention(transactions) {` ->
`+ if forcedByKind || usesCreditCardSignConvention(transactions) {`) and a comment. The
function body, both sample gates, and the bank-only-signal abort are byte-identical to
HEAD. So "neither more nor less eager for other kinds" holds by construction: when
forcedByKind is false the expression reduces to the original predicate.

I did not take that on faith. INDEPENDENT EXECUTION, in the scratchpad copy, asserting the
literal identity key rather than a relation between two computed values:

  sha256("2026-02-01|wegmans|50.00")[:8]  = 34e9f6c82bce222d   (pre-flip)
  sha256("2026-02-01|wegmans|-50.00")[:8] = 1ff5d7db5ad208bf   (post-flip)
  loadCSVFileForAccount(card.csv, {Kind: credit}) yields:
      amount=-50  hash=1ff5d7db5ad208bf  accountID="the-card"

The stored Hash is the post-flip hash, computed from hex I derived myself, not from
ComputeHash(). Identity is keyed to the amount the app actually uses.

MUTATION TESTING — seven defects injected one at a time into the scratchpad copy; each row
is a real `go test -count=1` run:

  M1  forcedByKind forced to false (override removed)
      -> FAIL TestCreditKind_ForcesFlipOnFileTooSmallForHeuristic/credit_forces_the_flip
      -> FAIL TestCreditKind_ForcedFlipRecomputesHash                          CAUGHT
  M2  post-flip `Hash = ComputeHash()` line deleted
      -> FAIL TestCreditKind_ForcedFlipRecomputesHash
      -> FAIL TestLoadCSVFile_HashReflectsPostFlipAmount (pre-existing test)   CAUGHT
  M3  `AccountID = acct.ID` stamping deleted
      -> FAIL x4 tests / 5 subtests                                            CAUGHT
  M4  override made over-eager (fires for EVERY kind)
      -> FAIL .../checking_leaves_the_heuristic_alone, savings_, brokerage_,
              other_leaves_the_heuristic_alone                                 CAUGHT
  M5  the two early-exit `setUnassignedCount(0)` calls deleted
      -> ok  (both packages green)                                             NOT CAUGHT
  M6  MatchFile iterates the raw slice instead of sortedByID
      -> FAIL TestMatchFile (3 subtests), TestMatchFile_DeterministicRegardless-
              OfSliceOrder, TestSaveWithWarnings_OverlappingPatternsWarnButSave CAUGHT
  M7  missing accounts.json treated as an error
      -> FAIL TestLoad_MissingFileIsNotAnError                                 CAUGHT

Six of seven caught, including every mutation on the sign path. M4 is the one that matters
most and is the answer to "would this pass with the override removed": the test does not
merely check that credit flips, it drives the SAME four-row fixture through all five kinds
and pins four negative cases, so both an absent override (M1) and an over-broad one (M4)
are caught in opposite directions.

The test is also self-guarding against silent decay. account_attribution_test.go:155-160
asserts the premise before asserting the behavior:

  if usesCreditCardSignConvention(raw) {
      t.Fatal("premise broken: the heuristic fires on this file, so the override proves nothing")
  }
  if raw[0].Amount != 50.00 { t.Fatalf("premise broken: unowned file was flipped ...") }

If the heuristic's thresholds ever move so that the 4-row fixture starts firing on its own,
the test fails loudly instead of quietly passing for the wrong reason. That is exactly the
failure mode ruling 2026-08-16a was about, and it is defended against here.

M5 is a genuine test gap, NOT an implementation defect: the implementation does reset the
count on both early-exit paths (loader.go, `dl.setUnassignedCount(0)` before each of the
two empty-set returns), and the normal-path staleness case IS covered by
TestUnassignedCount_AllAssignedAndNoAccountsConfigured (2 -> 0 across two loads). Only the
"previous load had unassigned rows, this load finds zero CSV files" ordering is untested,
because TestUnassignedCount_NoCSVFiles uses a fresh loader whose count is already 0.
Reporting this as a note, not a failure: the shipped code is correct, the brief's required
test list does not name this case, and no user-visible behavior is wrong.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. ATTRIBUTION, UNASSIGNED COUNTING, MatchFile DETERMINISM — PASS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
loader.go, per-file loop: `acct := accounts.Find(accts, accounts.MatchFile(accts, filename))`
then `dl.loadCSVFileForAccount(file, acct)`. On error the loop `continue`s with a warning
(pre-existing behavior, unchanged); a nil acct is NOT an error path — the file loads with
AccountID "" and is tallied in unassignedFiles. Never dropped.

Counting is done over the FINAL post-dedup set (`for i := range allTransactions { if
allTransactions[i].AccountID == "" { unassigned++ } }`), so the banner number reconciles
against the ledger the user is looking at. Executed evidence:
TestLoadData_StampsAccountIDAndCountsUnassigned asserts 3 stamped rows from the matched
file, 2 rows present-and-empty from the unmatched file, and UnassignedCount()==2. M3
breaks it.

MatchFile determinism: sortedByID copies before sorting (accounts.go:234-239), so the
caller's slice is never reordered — pinned by TestMatchFile_DoesNotReorderCallerSlice, and
TestMatchFile_DeterministicRegardlessOfSliceOrder feeds [a,b] and [b,a] and requires "aaa"
both ways. M6 breaks both. Matching is case-insensitive path.Match with substring fallback,
and a malformed glob (path.Match's only error is ErrBadPattern) falls through to substring
rather than failing the load — covered by the "malformed glob falls back to substring"
subtree.

accounts.Load failure inside LoadDataContext is non-fatal (`log.Printf("Warning: could not
load accounts: %v"); accts = nil`), verified end-to-end by
TestLoadData_CorruptAccountsFileStillLoads: a `{broken` sidecar still yields 2 loaded rows
and UnassignedCount()==2.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. MISSING / CORRUPT accounts.json — PASS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
accounts.go:51-57: os.IsNotExist(err) -> `return []models.Account{}, nil`. Not nil, not an
error. TestLoad_MissingFileIsNotAnError asserts both len==0 AND non-nil slice; M7 catches
its removal. Corrupt content IS an error at the accounts layer
(TestLoad_CorruptFileIsAnError) but is degraded to "everything unassigned" at the loader
layer — the correct split, since the loader must never fail a load over a sidecar.
Save-time validation rejects duplicate IDs, empty IDs and empty/whitespace names, and
TestSave_RejectsDuplicateIDsAndEmptyNames additionally asserts accounts.json was NOT
written on rejection (no partial write).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. SCOPE AND PIPELINE ORDER — PASS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
grep for StableID | TransferClass | TransferPairKey | BalanceAt | Suppressed |
transfer_decisions across all six manifest files: no hits. No new handler, template, route,
or MCP tool is in the manifest. A1/A3/A4/A5/A6-A9 are absent.

No other loader stage was reordered. Stage call sequence, HEAD vs working tree:

  HEAD  288 filterInternalTransfers  289 ClassifyTransactions  290 deduplicateTransactions
        295 applyDuplicateDetection  298 applyAliases  301 applyMajorExpenseNames
        304 applyAmazonEnrichment
  WT    330 filterInternalTransfers  331 ClassifyTransactions  332 deduplicateTransactions
        337 applyDuplicateDetection  340 applyAliases  343 applyMajorExpenseNames
        346 applyAmazonEnrichment

Identical order; only line numbers shift. All 14 diff hunks in loader.go are additive or
confined to the three intended sites.

Against design §2, A2's slice of the pipeline is parse -> sign-flip (heuristic + credit
override) -> account stamp, and stamping must precede dedup. Both hold: the flip and the
stamp are consecutive blocks at the end of loadCSVFileForAccount (flip first, stamp
second — the order the brief requires so identity is keyed to the post-flip amount), and
deduplicateTransactions runs later in LoadDataContext. The design's larger reordering
(StableID assignment, dedup before transfer classification, filterInternalTransfers ->
transfer classification) is explicitly A1/A3 work and is correctly ABSENT here; attempting
it would have been the scope violation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTES (non-blocking)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
a) Test gap, not a bug: the early-exit reset of unassignedCount (M5) has no test. A load
   that follows an unassigned load and finds zero CSV files would report a stale count if
   that line were ever deleted, and nothing would go red.
b) The oracle probe's credit-flip check is vacuously satisfiable on an empty result set
   (see item 3). The worker's own test is stronger; the oracle is the weaker artifact.
c) Confirming the carry-forward the wt-local worker flagged: explorer/handlers.go:477
   builds dataloader.New(importDir, store) where the CSV directory is not store.BaseDir(),
   while accounts.Load reads accounts.json from store.BaseDir(). Folder-import files are
   therefore matched against the user's real accounts. Correct today; A6/A7 must not
   assume CSV dir == data dir.

CONCLUSION: PASS. Every pinned symbol matches the brief exactly, the full toolchain and the
10-check oracle are green from my own execution, the credit-kind override is correct in
both directions and its hash consequence is confirmed against an independently derived
sha256, and six of seven injected defects — including all four on the sign/identity path —
are caught by the shipped tests.
